-- postpartum week 132: Nutrition-related health checks worth keeping on your radar (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   132,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Nutrition-related health checks worth keeping on your radar',
   '"A handful of nutrition-related terms — iron, vitamin B12, vitamin D, thyroid function — sometimes come up in routine bloodwork this far into postpartum life; knowing what they''re for helps you follow the conversation with your own clinician, not a checklist to schedule on your own."',
   'A handful of nutrition-related terms — iron, vitamin B12, vitamin D, thyroid function — sometimes come up in routine bloodwork this far into postpartum life. This week is a brief, plain-language reminder of what each is for, pointing back to this series'' own dedicated week on the topic where one exists, so the vocabulary is familiar if your clinician raises it. This isn''t a testing schedule or a self-diagnosis tool — which checks make sense for you, and when, is a judgment your own clinician makes based on your history and symptoms, not something this series can or should decide for you.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If it''s been a while since your last routine bloodwork: mention any fatigue, hair changes, or other symptoms to your clinician, who can decide whether checking iron, B12, vitamin D or thyroid function makes sense for you specifically — this series doesn''t set a testing schedule. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Andaman Islands home cooking, as prepared by the Bengali, Tamil, Telugu and Karen settler communities who make up most of the islands'' population — coconut-based curries, fresh seafood, and tropical produce like tapioca, jackfruit and banana flower. If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This week isn''t about ordering tests for yourself — it''s about recognising a few nutrition-related terms your clinician might mention, so the conversation makes sense if and when it comes up. None of this replaces your own care team''s judgment about what''s relevant for you specifically.", "breakfast_a": "Tapioca-and-vegetable stew with a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stew) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jackfruit curry, coconut-based (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Coconut fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rajma-and-vegetable khichdi with banana flower (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked banana flower; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Chicken coconut curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Iron deficiency: when assessment may help", "notes": "This far into postpartum life, iron deficiency isn''t automatically something to attribute to childbirth — it can persist, resolve, or develop fresh for unrelated reasons, and heavy bleeding at delivery or closely spaced pregnancies are examples of risk factors, not an explanation on their own. Fatigue alone isn''t a reliable sign either way — it''s worth having assessed when it''s indicated by your symptoms or history, not assumed one way or the other. If your clinician suggests checking, it''s a simple blood test, and food choices alone don''t replace a supplement if one''s indicated. This series covered iron in its own dedicated weeks earlier on.", "breakfast_a": "Banana-flower stir-fry with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Tapioca-and-vegetable stew with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stew, 1 cup rice) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Prawn coconut curry (100g prawns) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Andaman-style chicken pepper fry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Vitamin B12, especially on a vegetarian or vegan diet", "notes": "Dairy and eggs contribute B12, but eating them doesn''t automatically guarantee adequate levels — absorption problems, which become more common with age and can affect anyone regardless of diet, are a separate route to low B12 that a plant-based diet alone doesn''t explain and eating animal-source food alone doesn''t rule out. Unusual fatigue, tingling or numbness, or other new symptoms are worth mentioning to your clinician regardless of what you eat. Worth knowing: If you eat little or no animal-source food, vitamin B12 is a genuinely useful one to ask your clinician about — this series covered it in its own dedicated week, and it isn''t something food alone reliably covers on a strict vegetarian or vegan diet.", "breakfast_a": "Jackfruit curry with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup curry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken coconut curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer coconut curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Vitamin D, a quick reminder", "notes": "This series also covered vitamin D in its own dedicated weeks — worth a brief reminder here since it''s one of the more commonly checked nutrition-related labs. Routine testing isn''t automatically needed for otherwise healthy adults taking ordinary amounts; it becomes more relevant if a deficiency is suspected or already diagnosed.", "breakfast_a": "Tapioca-and-vegetable stew with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stew, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken coconut curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Jackfruit curry with toor dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-coconut curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Thyroid function, if your history includes it", "notes": "If you had a thyroid condition identified during pregnancy or postpartum, ongoing monitoring is usually already part of your care plan — this series covered eating well through a postpartum thyroid swing in its own dedicated week. If you haven''t had a thyroid diagnosis, new or persistent symptoms — unusual fatigue, weight change, hair or mood changes, among others — can still be worth discussing with your clinician; a previous diagnosis isn''t a requirement for raising the conversation.", "breakfast_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils and vegetables (1 cup dal-vegetable mix) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jackfruit curry with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup curry, 1 cup rice) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken coconut curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "These checks follow your own history, not a fixed schedule", "notes": "None of this week''s mentions are things this series is telling you to schedule — they''re here so the terms are familiar if your clinician raises them, based on your own history and symptoms, not a generic postpartum checklist everyone follows the same way.", "breakfast_a": "Banana-flower stir-fry with drumstick and rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with capsicum (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Tapioca-and-vegetable stew with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stew, 1 cup rice) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken coconut curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back across this run of weeks, is there a nutrition-related term from this week you''d feel more confident asking your clinician about now?", "breakfast_a": "Tapioca-and-vegetable stew with a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stew) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jackfruit curry, coconut-based (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Coconut fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rajma-and-vegetable khichdi with banana flower (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked banana flower; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Chicken coconut curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 133: Eating well when family gives you conflicting food advice (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   133,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when family gives you conflicting food advice',
   '"Family advice may be well meant, but it can also feel pressuring — you can choose what suits you and set boundaries around your own meals without having to justify every choice."',
   'As your family settles into a longer routine with your child, food advice from grandparents, in-laws, aunts, uncles and neighbours doesn''t necessarily taper off — sometimes it''s about your child''s plate, but often it''s still about yours: what you should eat more of, less of, or avoid altogether. Much of this advice is offered with real affection, even when it''s outdated, contradicts current guidance, or doesn''t match what works for you — but that doesn''t mean it never feels like pressure, especially when it''s repeated often or tied to your child''s wellbeing. This week isn''t about which specific claims are right or wrong — it''s about how you relate to that advice: you''re allowed to listen without automatically complying, to decline without a lengthy justification, and to change your mind when something genuinely makes sense, on your own terms and timeline.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you''re managing a condition family members aren''t fully aware of: your own care team''s guidance takes priority over general advice from relatives, however well-intentioned — you''re not obliged to explain your medical situation to justify a food choice. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Rajasthani/Marwari home cooking (bajra roti, gatte ki sabzi, ker sangri, laal maas, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Family food advice after having a baby is common almost everywhere. It can come from a genuine wish to help, even when it doesn''t match current guidance or your own preferences — though it can also feel like pressure, especially when it''s frequent or insistent. Either way, you don''t have to accept advice to be polite about it, and you don''t owe anyone a detailed justification to decline it.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with onion and tomato (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gatte ki sabzi, gram-flour dumplings in a spiced yogurt curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup dumplings) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a ker sangri sabzi", "lunch_b": "Laal maas, Rajasthani red mutton curry (100g mutton) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a ker sangri sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted moong dal namkeen (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Wheat roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with wheat roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "When the advice conflicts with your own care team''s guidance", "notes": "Food advice varies in usefulness and safety. Advice about your medical condition, supplements or medication timing should follow your individual care plan. Worth knowing: If a family member''s food advice conflicts with something your doctor, dietitian or lactation consultant has specifically told you, follow your own care team — they know your individual situation, test results and history in a way a well-meaning relative usually doesn''t. It''s fine to say so plainly: \"My doctor''s asked me to do it this way for now.\"", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with grated carrot (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Ker sangri sabzi with wheat khichdi (1 cup khichdi, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked cracked wheat and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal) with bajra roti (2 pieces)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Marwari-style (100g chicken) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a ker sangri sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Wheat roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Marwari-style (2 eggs) with wheat roti (2 pieces) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "When advice comes with pressure to eat more, or less", "notes": "\"Eat more, you need it for the baby\" and \"don''t eat that, it''s too heavy\" can both show up in the same week, sometimes from the same person. How much you eat is still your own call, guided by your own appetite and any care plan you follow — you can thank someone for their concern and still serve yourself the portion that''s right for you.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with peanuts, onion and curry leaves (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gatte ki sabzi (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup dumplings) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a gawar phali sabzi", "lunch_b": "Safed maas, Rajasthani white mutton curry (100g mutton) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a gawar phali sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-vegetable khichdi with carrots and peas (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus the vegetables) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry, Marwari-style (100g chicken) with wheat roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Traditional postpartum-diet beliefs", "notes": "Many families carry specific beliefs about foods that help or harm milk supply, healing or the baby''s temperament — some line up with current evidence, some don''t. You can enjoy a food because it''s meaningful to your family without needing it to have a proven physiological effect, and skipping one is a reasonable choice too, without needing to predict exactly what difference it will or won''t make.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with capsicum (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with spinach (2 eggs) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Ker sangri sabzi with chana dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal) with bajra roti (2 pieces)", "lunch_b": "Laal maas, Rajasthani red mutton curry (100g mutton) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Wheat roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with wheat roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Setting a boundary without turning it into a conflict", "notes": "You don''t need a debate to decline advice or a dish that doesn''t suit you — a short, warm line usually does the job: \"That sounds good, I''ll keep it in mind,\" or \"I''m doing it this way for now, but thank you.\" Repeating the same calm response, without justifying it further each time, is often what actually ends the conversation.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with grated bottle gourd (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gatte ki sabzi (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup dumplings) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Rajasthani-style (100g fish) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Wheat roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Marwari-style (2 eggs) with wheat roti (2 pieces) and a bhindi sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s actually worth changing your mind", "notes": "Not all advice is worth dismissing either — sometimes a relative''s suggestion is genuinely useful, or points to something worth asking your own care team about. Weighing advice on what it actually says, rather than on who said it or how strongly, works both ways: it''s fine to take it seriously, and just as fine to set it aside.", "breakfast_a": "Dalia, cracked wheat porridge with mixed vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Ker sangri sabzi with moong dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal) with bajra roti (2 pieces)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Marwari-style (100g chicken) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a gawar phali sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi with cauliflower and beans (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus the vegetables) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with wheat roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Was there a piece of advice from someone this week that you decided was actually worth keeping — or one you were glad to set aside?", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with onion and tomato (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gatte ki sabzi, gram-flour dumplings in a spiced yogurt curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup dumplings) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a ker sangri sabzi", "lunch_b": "Laal maas, Rajasthani red mutton curry (100g mutton) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a ker sangri sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted moong dal namkeen (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Wheat roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with wheat roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
