-- postpartum week 134: Niacin (vitamin B3), and where it fits into your everyday meals (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   134,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Niacin (vitamin B3), and where it fits into your everyday meals',
   '"Niacin helps your body convert food into usable energy, and comes from a wide range of everyday foods — chicken, fish, mushrooms, peanuts, whole grains and legumes — plus a real, if partial, contribution your body makes by converting dietary tryptophan; deficiency is rare with a varied diet, though high-dose supplements are a different matter, can affect blood glucose as well as the liver, and belong under a clinician''s supervision."',
   'Niacin (vitamin B3) is one of the B vitamins your body relies on to convert food into usable energy. It''s found across a wide range of everyday foods — chicken, fish, mushrooms, peanuts, whole grains and legumes — and your body also converts some dietary tryptophan (from protein-rich foods generally) into niacin, a real, if partial, contribution to your overall intake, not merely an irrelevant backup. For most people eating a varied diet, this need is met without any deliberate tracking. High-dose niacin supplements, sometimes prescribed for cholesterol management, are a different situation entirely — they can cause flushing and, at higher doses, affect liver function and raise blood glucose (particularly relevant if you manage diabetes), and belong under a clinician''s supervision. That''s separate from anything in this week''s food-based guidance, which needs no special caution for most people.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you take a prescribed niacin supplement for cholesterol management, or you manage diabetes: follow your clinician''s dosing and monitoring plan — high-dose niacin can affect blood glucose as well as the liver, so this is a different situation from niacin in food, and isn''t something to adjust based on general guidance like this week''s. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Kashmiri Pandit home cooking (nadru/lotus-stem yakhni, haak, dum aloo, rogan josh, tomato chaman, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Niacin is one of the B vitamins your body uses to convert the food you eat into usable energy. It''s found in a wide range of everyday foods, and most people eating a varied diet meet their needs without any special planning or tracking.", "breakfast_a": "Nadru, lotus-stem yakhni-style curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a haak saag", "lunch_b": "Rogan josh, Kashmiri mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a haak saag", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Tomato chaman, paneer in a tomato-based curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Everyday food sources, and the body''s own conversion", "notes": "Chicken, fish, mushrooms, peanuts and whole grains are all reliable niacin sources. Your body also converts some of the dietary tryptophan you get from protein-rich foods generally into niacin — a real, if partial, contribution to your total intake, not just an irrelevant backup to the foods that contain niacin directly.", "breakfast_a": "Nadru yakhni with mushrooms (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dum aloo with chana dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a haak saag", "lunch_b": "Kashmiri trout curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a haak saag", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a mushroom sabzi", "dinner_b": "Kokur, Kashmiri chicken yakhni-style curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If you take a niacin supplement for cholesterol", "notes": "If you or someone in your household takes a prescribed niacin supplement, that''s a medical decision your clinician is already managing — this week''s food-based guidance is a separate, much gentler picture, and doesn''t call for the same caution. Worth knowing: High-dose niacin supplements, sometimes prescribed for cholesterol management, are a genuinely different situation from niacin in food — they can cause flushing, itching and, at high doses, affect liver function and raise blood glucose, which is particularly relevant if you manage diabetes. They need to be started and monitored by a clinician. This isn''t a concern with niacin from ordinary meals.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, cracked wheat porridge with mixed vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a dum aloo", "lunch_b": "Rogan josh (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mushroom sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi with carrots and peas (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus the vegetables) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Tomato chaman (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a haak saag", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Deficiency is uncommon with a varied diet", "notes": "Niacin deficiency (historically known as pellagra) is uncommon where diets include a mix of protein sources, whole grains and legumes — it was historically linked to diets built almost entirely around untreated corn, which is low in usable niacin. If your meals already include some variety across the week, this isn''t something you need to actively track.", "breakfast_a": "Nadru yakhni with carrots (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Haak saag with moong dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Kokur yakhni-style curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Kashmiri-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mushroom sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Good options if you eat mostly vegetarian", "notes": "Mushrooms, peanuts, whole grains, legumes and dairy all contribute niacin without needing meat, fish or eggs — a plant-forward day can meet this need comfortably as long as it includes some variety across these groups.", "breakfast_a": "Nadru yakhni with mushrooms and peas (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dum aloo with rajma (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a haak saag", "lunch_b": "Kashmiri trout curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a mushroom sabzi", "dinner_b": "Tomato chaman with peas (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one meal", "notes": "One meal without an obvious niacin source, or one particularly protein-rich day, doesn''t meaningfully change your status either way — as with most of the nutrients covered in this series, it''s the overall pattern across the week that matters, not any single plate.", "breakfast_a": "Semolina upma with mixed vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma with dum aloo (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Rogan josh (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans-and-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-vegetable khichdi with mushrooms and peas (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus the vegetables) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Kokur yakhni-style curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back across this run of weeks, which single change has made the most noticeable difference to how you feel day to day?", "breakfast_a": "Nadru, lotus-stem yakhni-style curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a haak saag", "lunch_b": "Rogan josh, Kashmiri mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a haak saag", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Tomato chaman, paneer in a tomato-based curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 135: Eating well as you return to work outside the home (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   135,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well as you return to work outside the home',
   '"A tighter morning, a workday away from your own kitchen and an evening still full of childcare all make eating well harder logistically, not less important — a little planning around what travels well, what''s quick, and what you can eat at your desk goes further than trying to eat perfectly."',
   'Returning to paid work outside the home, whether for the first time since your baby arrived or after a longer stretch, reshuffles when and how you eat far more than what you''re actually capable of eating well. Mornings get tighter, lunch depends on what''s available where you work, and evenings often start a second round of childcare and household tasks right when you''re already tired. None of this makes eating well impossible — it just calls for a bit more planning around what travels, what reheats, and what you can eat quickly without much preparation. This week''s meals lean toward options that hold up outside a home kitchen.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If your medication needs specific timing around meals: build that timing into your new work schedule deliberately rather than letting it slip on busy days — a phone reminder can help until the new routine settles in. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Andhra/coastal Telugu home cooking (pappu, gongura pachadi, pulusu, pesarattu, gutti vankaya, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Returning to work outside the home changes the logistics of eating far more than the underlying need — you still need regular, adequate meals, just fit around a schedule that isn''t entirely your own anymore. This week is about practical adjustments, not a stricter standard.", "breakfast_a": "Pesarattu, moong dal dosa (2 medium) with ginger chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Pappu, Andhra dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gutti vankaya", "lunch_b": "Andhra chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gutti vankaya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Quick mornings that still cover the basics", "notes": "A rushed morning doesn''t have to mean skipping breakfast — a dosa or paratha made the night before and reheated, boiled eggs prepped in advance, or even a glass of milk with fruit on the way out are all reasonable options when there isn''t time for a full sit-down meal.", "breakfast_a": "Pesarattu with onion (2 medium) with ginger chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gongura pappu, sorrel-leaf dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Andhra mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked), a small portion of gongura pachadi (2 tbsp) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Andhra-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Eating enough through a meeting-heavy day", "notes": "It''s easy for lunch to slide later and later on a busy day, or to be skipped altogether. Keeping something portable on hand for exactly those days is a practical safeguard, not a sign you''re not managing well. Worth knowing: If work meetings routinely push your meals very late, a packed snack — nuts, fruit, a boiled egg — that doesn''t depend on a break room or canteen timing can help prevent long stretches without food, which often show up later as low energy or feeling intensely hungry later. Keep a boiled egg and any other perishable snack refrigerated, or in an insulated bag with frozen cold packs, and refrigerate it on arrival where possible — discard it if it''s been left without temperature control for more than two hours, or one hour above 32°C.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (4 pieces) with sambar and coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Pappu with gutti vankaya (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Fish pulusu, Andhra tamarind fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gutti vankaya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-vegetable khichdi with carrots and peas (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus the vegetables) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Andhra chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Staying hydrated and snacking well at a desk", "notes": "A desk job can make it easy to forget water for hours at a stretch — keeping a bottle within sight is a simple reminder. A handful of nuts or fruit kept at your desk is a convenient option between meals. Curd is a good option too, but only if it can stay refrigerated or in an insulated bag with a frozen cold pack — an insulated bag alone, without a cold pack, isn''t enough, and refrigerating it again after it''s been out too long doesn''t make it safe again.", "breakfast_a": "Pesarattu with grated bottle gourd (2 medium) with ginger chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gongura pappu, sorrel-leaf dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Andhra chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked), a small portion of gongura pachadi (2 tbsp) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Office food culture — canteens, treats and potlucks", "notes": "Cake for a colleague''s birthday, a canteen tray that isn''t quite what you''d cook at home, or a team lunch you didn''t plan for are all a normal part of office life. None of it needs to derail the rest of your day — enjoy what you choose to, and pick up your usual pattern at the next meal.", "breakfast_a": "Pesarattu with methi (2 medium) with ginger chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Pappu (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gutti vankaya", "lunch_b": "Prawn curry, Andhra-style (100g prawns) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gutti vankaya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Andhra-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The evening second shift — keeping dinner simple", "notes": "Coming home after a full workday to childcare, cooking and everything else waiting doesn''t leave much energy for an elaborate dinner. A simple, repeatable dinner — dal, rice, a vegetable, curd — is a completely adequate choice on these days, not a compromise.", "breakfast_a": "Semolina upma with mixed vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gongura pappu, sorrel-leaf dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Fish pulusu (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked), a small portion of gongura pachadi (2 tbsp) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi with cauliflower and beans (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus the vegetables) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Andhra mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. What''s one part of your new work routine that''s still settling — and one small change that might make eating through it easier?", "breakfast_a": "Pesarattu, moong dal dosa (2 medium) with ginger chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Pappu, Andhra dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gutti vankaya", "lunch_b": "Andhra chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gutti vankaya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 136: Pantothenic acid (vitamin B5), one of the more overlooked B vitamins (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   136,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Pantothenic acid (vitamin B5), one of the more overlooked B vitamins',
   '"Pantothenic acid helps your body release energy from food, and its name literally means "from everywhere" in Greek — it''s present in nearly every food group, which makes a genuine dietary deficiency rare; you don''t need to track it, and a dedicated supplement isn''t something most people need."',
   'Pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) is part of a coenzyme your body uses constantly to release energy from the food you eat. Its name comes from the Greek for "from everywhere," reflecting how widely it''s distributed — grains, legumes, vegetables, dairy, eggs, meat, mushrooms and more all contribute small amounts. A genuine dietary deficiency is rare outside severe malnutrition. You may see pantothenic acid, or the related compound dexpanthenol, marketed in skin and hair products. Most of the supporting evidence relates to topical use, not to eating more of it than a typical varied diet already supplies — this week''s meals need no special adjustment either way.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you take a prenatal, postnatal or general multivitamin: check what it already contains before adding a separate B-vitamin supplement — combined products can add up unnoticed, and most people don''t need an additional pantothenic acid supplement on top of one. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Gujarati/Kathiyawadi home cooking (thepla, undhiyu, kadhi, khaman, handvo, dal dhokli, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) is another of the B vitamins your body uses to release energy from food, working as part of a coenzyme your cells use constantly. Its name comes from the Greek for \"from everywhere,\" because it''s present in small amounts across nearly every food group — grains, legumes, vegetables, dairy, eggs and meat all contribute.", "breakfast_a": "Methi thepla (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g) and a pickle", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gujarati kadhi with khichdi (1 cup khichdi, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal and a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice) with an undhiyu", "lunch_b": "Kathiyawadi chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an undhiyu", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Thepla (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with thepla (2 pieces) and a bhindi sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Everyday food sources, without needing to seek them out", "notes": "Mushrooms, avocado, sunflower seeds, eggs, whole grains and legumes are all particularly good sources, but because pantothenic acid is so widely distributed, most people meet their needs through ordinary meals without deliberately including any of these.", "breakfast_a": "Handvo, a savoury baked lentil-and-vegetable cake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal dhokli, wheat dumplings in a sweet-and-tangy dal (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with an undhiyu", "lunch_b": "Kathiyawadi mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an undhiyu", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Thepla (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a mushroom sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Kathiyawadi-style (2 eggs) with thepla (2 pieces) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Deficiency is rare", "notes": "Because pantothenic acid is so widely present in food, a genuine dietary deficiency is rare on an ordinary varied diet, even one that isn''t perfectly balanced — this isn''t a nutrient that calls for special attention, and testing should be based on individual indications rather than routine.", "breakfast_a": "Khaman dhokla (2 pieces) with green chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Undhiyu with rice (1 cup cooked rice, plus a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked tuvar lilva in the undhiyu) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Kathiyawadi chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi with carrots and peas (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus the vegetables) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with thepla (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Skin and hair supplement claims, kept in perspective", "notes": "You may see pantothenic acid, or a related compound called dexpanthenol, marketed in skin and hair products or supplements. Most of that evidence relates to topical use, not to eating more pantothenic acid than a typical diet already provides — extra dietary intake beyond what you already get from food isn''t shown to add a skin or hair benefit on its own.", "breakfast_a": "Methi thepla with grated carrot (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gujarati kadhi with rice (1 cup rice) and a dal dhokli (includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal)", "lunch_b": "Kathiyawadi mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Thepla (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Kathiyawadi-style (2 eggs) with thepla (2 pieces) and a mushroom sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you already take a prenatal or postnatal multivitamin", "notes": "Most prenatal and postnatal multivitamins already include pantothenic acid alongside other B vitamins — you don''t need a separate supplement on top of one you''re already taking, and combined products can add up unnoticed if you''re not tracking what''s in each one.", "breakfast_a": "Handvo with grated bottle gourd (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Undhiyu with khichdi (1 cup khichdi, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal and a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice)", "lunch_b": "Veraval-style pomfret curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an undhiyu", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Thepla (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with thepla (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one meal", "notes": "As with most of the nutrients this series covers, one meal without an obvious source doesn''t change anything meaningfully — pantothenic acid''s presence across nearly every food group means the overall pattern of eating regular, varied meals through the week already takes care of this one.", "breakfast_a": "Khaman dhokla with grated cabbage (2 pieces) with green chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal dhokli (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with an undhiyu", "lunch_b": "Kathiyawadi chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans-and-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-vegetable khichdi with mushrooms and peas (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus the vegetables) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Kathiyawadi-style (2 eggs) with thepla (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back across this run of weeks, which single change has made the most noticeable difference to how you feel day to day?", "breakfast_a": "Methi thepla (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g) and a pickle", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gujarati kadhi with khichdi (1 cup khichdi, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal and a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice) with an undhiyu", "lunch_b": "Kathiyawadi chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an undhiyu", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Thepla (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with thepla (2 pieces) and a bhindi sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
