-- postpartum week 137: Eating well while you're pumping or expressing milk at work (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   137,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well while you''re pumping or expressing milk at work',
   '"Pumping at work adds its own logistics — extra fluid, a snack within reach during a hands-limited session, breaks that need to double as actual meals — but not a fundamentally different eating pattern from the rest of this series; a little planning around the routine matters more than any specific "supply food.""',
   'If you are still breastfeeding or expressing milk, this week''s guidance may help. If not, the meals below remain usable on their own, and you can simply skip the pumping-related notes — nothing here assumes every mother at this stage has the same additional requirements. If you''re pumping or expressing milk during your workday, the underlying nutrition picture is the same one this series has covered throughout — regular meals, adequate fluid, a dependable protein and calcium source through the day, adjusted to your own appetite, activity and however much you''re currently expressing. What''s different is the logistics: shorter windows, hands that are often occupied, and breaks that need to cover both a pumping session and something to eat. There''s no special "pumping diet," and no single food that reliably changes your supply. A little planning — water at your pumping station, a snack prepped in advance and kept cold, pairing a session with a meal where your schedule allows — tends to help more than chasing a particular ingredient.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you''re concerned your supply has genuinely dropped: a lactation consultant or your own clinician can help identify the actual cause — it''s rarely a single food, and troubleshooting it yourself based on diet alone often isn''t the most useful approach. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Mangalorean/Tulu Nadu home cooking (neer dosa, kori gassi, alsande sukka, pundi, ghee roast, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Pumping or expressing at work fits into the same general eating pattern this series has covered all along — regular meals, enough fluid, some protein and calcium through the day. What changes is the logistics: less time, less flexibility about when you eat, and sessions that need planning around.", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa (3 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Alsande sukka, cowpea stir-fry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked cowpeas) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Kori gassi, Mangalorean chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Extra fluid, kept simple", "notes": "Breastfeeding and pumping both increase your fluid needs somewhat, and a workday can make it easy to lose track of how much you''ve actually had. Keeping a filled water bottle at your pumping station, alongside your milk and equipment, is a simple habit that removes the need to remember separately.", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa with grated coconut (3 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Pundi, rice dumplings (3 pieces) with an alsande sukka (includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked cowpeas) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mangalorean mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Mangalorean-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "\"Supply foods\" and why most of this is a myth", "notes": "It''s easy to feel pressure to eat or drink something specific to \"keep supply up.\" For most people, that pressure isn''t backed by strong evidence — a generally adequate, varied diet does the job just as well as any specialised product. Worth knowing: Foods and drinks marketed to boost milk supply — lactation cookies, specific teas, particular grains — don''t have strong evidence behind them for most people, and skipping them doesn''t put your supply at risk. Adequate overall intake and effective, regular removal of milk (through feeding or pumping) matter far more than any single food.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (4 pieces) with sambar and coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Mooga sukka (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Ghee roast chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Pundi, rice dumplings (3 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot-peas sabzi", "dinner_b": "Kori gassi (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A snack within reach during the session itself", "notes": "Pumping sessions often leave your hands occupied, which makes eating mid-session impractical. Prepping something you can eat just before or right after — nuts, a boiled egg, fruit already cut — is more realistic than trying to snack while pumping itself. Keep a boiled egg or cut fruit refrigerated, or in an insulated bag with a frozen cold pack, and discard it if it''s been left without temperature control for more than two hours, or one hour above 32°C.", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa with jaggery (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Pundi, rice dumplings (3 pieces) with an alsande sukka (includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked cowpeas) and a spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mangalorean fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Coordinating pumping breaks with actual meals", "notes": "When pumping breaks and meal breaks compete for the same short window, meals can end up skipped entirely. Where your schedule allows it, pairing a pumping session with a meal or snack — rather than treating them as two separate demands on the same block of time — can make both easier to fit in.", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa with cardamom (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Mooga sukka (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Prawn gassi, Mangalorean prawn curry (100g prawns) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Mangalorean-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Supply fluctuates for many reasons, not just food", "notes": "A lower-output pumping session doesn''t necessarily mean you ate the wrong thing that day — stress, how recently you last fed or pumped, time of day, and plenty of other factors all play a role. It''s worth resisting the urge to trace a single session back to a single meal.", "breakfast_a": "Semolina upma with mixed vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Alsande sukka with mooga sukka (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked cowpeas) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans-and-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Kori gassi (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans-and-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Neer dosa (3 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Mangalorean mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. What''s one small change to your pumping-and-eating routine this week that made the day feel a little less rushed?", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa (3 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Alsande sukka, cowpea stir-fry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked cowpeas) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Kori gassi, Mangalorean chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
